# GB10 unified-memory budget: the ONE place every launcher takes its numbers from.
# (Installed to ~/ufo-galaxy/ by install.sh; /srv/models/gx10_budget.env, if it exists,
# wins so SparkDeck's profiles and our scripts can share a single file.)
#
# Why a budget at all: the gx10 has 121 GB of unified memory shared by CPU and GPU, no
# separate VRAM to absorb a mistake. Three controllers (SparkDeck, the watchdog, our
# scripts) each had their own idea of "how much to give Qwen": 0.35, 0.56 and 0.70, with
# Venus at 0.19/0.20/0.26. Any two of those together (0.70+0.20 = 109 GB) leave nothing for
# the OS, the page cache used while loading weights, sshd, tailscaled and dockerd - and the
# box stops answering SSH while still answering ping. That is the outage.
#
# Every value can still be overridden per call from the environment.

# Total unified memory, GB.
TOTAL_GB="${TOTAL_GB:-121}"

# Hard ceiling for the SUM of all vLLM pools (fraction of TOTAL_GB). 0.62 = ~75 GB of
# models; the remaining ~46 GB is for OS + page cache + services + any llama.cpp/Ollama
# process the stack manager adds (the 27B Q8 llama.cpp stack alone is ~30 GB + KV).
MODEL_POOL_MAX="${MODEL_POOL_MAX:-0.62}"

# Free-memory policy (percent of TOTAL_GB counted as MemAvailable: free + reclaimable cache).
#   FLOOR  (soft, enforced): free memory must never be allowed to fall below this. Launches that
#          would take it under are refused (even with GX10_FORCE); at runtime gx10_memguard.sh
#          sheds load and earlyoom acts at this level. "Soft" = we shed the cheap things first
#          and only refuse/kill at the floor; nothing is killed while free memory is above it.
#   TARGET (encouraged): launches that would leave less than this warn, --audit warns, and
#          gx10_memguard.sh starts releasing reclaimable memory (Ollama models, page cache,
#          OmniParser). GX10_STRICT=1 makes a launch under TARGET a refusal too.
FREE_FLOOR_PCT="${FREE_FLOOR_PCT:-5}"
FREE_TARGET_PCT="${FREE_TARGET_PCT:-10}"


# Qwen3.6-35B-A3B NVFP4 (vLLM, :8000, served as `qwen-abliterated`).
# 0.35 = ~42 GB: 23 GB weights + FP8 KV. It was 0.68 (82 GB) for a 23 GB model.
# SEQS is the number of requests decoded together (continuous batching). It was hard-coded
# to 1, so every user and every agent step queued behind the previous one. Decode is
# memory-bandwidth bound and only ~3B parameters are active per token, so a batch of 8 costs
# little per-stream speed and multiplies throughput for everyone sharing the box. This model
# is a hybrid (mostly linear-attention layers), so its KV cache is small and 8 streams fit in
# the same 0.35 pool. This is the ONLY Qwen left serving :8000; the llama.cpp 27B is retired
# (gx10_retire_qwen27b.sh).
# Optional vLLM flags, appended verbatim (untested combinations can stop the container from
# starting, so none are on by default): QWEN_EXTRA_ARGS='--async-scheduling'
QWEN_GPU_MEM="${QWEN_GPU_MEM:-0.35}"
QWEN_MAX_LEN="${QWEN_MAX_LEN:-65536}"
QWEN_SEQS="${QWEN_SEQS:-8}"

# UI-Venus-2-9B grounding (vLLM, :8002). bf16 weights ~19 GB; 0.20 = ~24 GB.
VENUS_GPU_MEM="${VENUS_GPU_MEM:-0.20}"
VENUS_MAX_LEN="${VENUS_MAX_LEN:-16384}"
VENUS_SEQS="${VENUS_SEQS:-8}"
# Opt-in speed-up: VENUS_QUANT=fp8 quantizes the bf16 weights on load (vLLM --quantization fp8):
# ~half the weight bytes read per token and ~9 GB freed, on a bandwidth-bound box. NOT default:
# it can shift pixel coordinates, so run bench_venus*.py against it before enabling.
VENUS_QUANT="${VENUS_QUANT:-}"

# Least-important auxiliary models, stopped one per pass (2 min apart) by gx10_memguard.sh only
# when free memory is UNDER the floor, in this order. The primary Qwen and Venus are never listed.
# Names that are not running are skipped.
SHED_CONTAINERS="${SHED_CONTAINERS:-qwen35-9b-defiant-q6 qwen3-0.6b}"
